zita-convolver: a level is running from start (), not from when its thread is first scheduled

Convlevel::start () creates a detached thread and returns; the thread sets
_stat = ST_PROC when it first runs. A Convproc::stop_process () and
cleanup () that come before the thread is scheduled therefore see the level
idle, skip waiting for it and free its buffers, and the thread then runs on
freed memory: SIGSEGV, or SIGABRT from malloc ("unaligned tcache chunk
detected"). guitarix's gx_amp, gx_cabinet and gxmetal_head hit this in the
lifecycle step (instantiate, activate, deactivate, free, no run between) at
192 kHz in 128-frame blocks, the cell with the most levels and so the most
threads: 10 of 10 runs crashed, 0 of 10 with this patch.

The level is marked running before pthread_create (), so a stop sees it and
waits; a pthread_create () that fails marks it idle again, as before the
patch, where readout () processes the level in line. The same change is in
the GrandOrgue project's maintained copy of zita-convolver (its commits
4967141 and 28dac3d, "Fix Convproc::cleanup() hang when torn down right
after start_process()").
https://github.com/Open-Mixer/plugin-qualify/issues/29

--- a/source/zita-convolver.cc
+++ b/source/zita-convolver.cc
@@ -621,7 +621,10 @@
     pthread_attr_setscope (&attr, PTHREAD_SCOPE_SYSTEM);
     pthread_attr_setinheritsched (&attr, PTHREAD_EXPLICIT_SCHED);
     pthread_attr_setstacksize (&attr, 0x10000);
-    pthread_create (&_pthr, &attr, static_main, this);
+    // The level runs from here, not from when its thread is first scheduled: a stop () and a cleanup () that come
+    // before the thread runs must see it running and wait for it, or they free what the thread then uses.
+    _stat = ST_PROC;
+    if (pthread_create (&_pthr, &attr, static_main, this)) _stat = ST_IDLE;
     pthread_attr_destroy (&attr);
 }

@@ -689,7 +692,6 @@

 void Convlevel::main (void)
 {
-    _stat = ST_PROC;
     while (true)
     {
 	_trig.wait ();
